Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Raw Burlap Strangles the Propeller
- Story & Context: When Doña Marta's produce barge snagged a floating tangle of wet coffee sacking near the Boca del Río locks, the sudden engine stall threatened to crush three wooden skiffs against the concrete sluice in the predawn gloom. Refusing to wait for maritime patrol, she fired up her two-stroke generator, slammed a heavily scratched jungle cassette into her deck, and keyed her horn mic to blast chopped 165-BPM breakbeats and screeching trumpet notes across the water to force oncoming boats backward. The surrounding grandmothers immediately recognized the warning cadence, chiming in over their own battery-powered horns with rapid-fire Spanish battle rhymes to direct traffic out of the undertow. As water churned brown against the gunwales, Marta locked eyes with the lockmaster and bellowed the track's defining battle cry: "Tira la soga, rompe el compás, que aquí nadie se va pa'trás."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Album artwork showing a tangled knot of fibrous burlap and submerged rope wrapping around a brass boat propeller beneath dark churned water, illuminated from above by the harsh incandescent glare of a market floodlight cutting through morning fog, high contrast, cinematic realism.

Track 2: Tell Me Which Seam Broke First
- Story & Context: Hidden under the weeping willows of Embarcadero Cuemanco, selector Rosa spent an hour mending torn sailcloth with an ivory needle while confiding into a cheap dynamic microphone why she had pitched her drum breaks into a manic, fractured gallop. The air smelled of swamp lilies and solder resin as she detailed her quiet estrangement from the canal syndicate, contrasting her whispered vocal delivery with an operatic, sorrowful trumpet solo played by her son from the boat's stern. When an unannounced inspector's skiff glided out of the darkness to search her cargo, she suddenly let the track drop into a thunderous 808 dembow roar, vibrating the inspector's hull until he retreated into the reeds without writing a single citation. Threading her twine through the last eyelet, Rosa spoke the track’s haunting hook with venomous composure: "Puntada por puntada cobro la cuenta, mi amor, que el río no perdona traición."
